                    <item>
                <title>One with the world? A new look at brains transformed by psychedelics.</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/the-psychedelic-brain-looks-like-chaos-underneath-theres-order/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/the-psychedelic-brain-looks-like-chaos-underneath-theres-order/#comments</comments>
                
                <pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 11:15:35 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category>Science</category>
		<category>brain</category>
		<category>Neuroscience</category>
		<category>psilocybin</category>
		<category>psychedelics</category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/the-psychedelic-brain-looks-like-chaos-underneath-theres-order/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        Participants meditated inside an MRI tube, with and without psilocybin.
                    </description>
                                                                <ns2:encoded>
                            &lt;p&gt;For about a decade, the mainstream understanding has been that psychedelics cause chaos in the brain. Brain networks—the stable patterns of communication that handle vision, attention, or our sense of self, and more—loosen and start talking to each other all at once. </ns2:encoded>
                                    
                                    <ns3:comments>90</ns3:comments>
                
                
                <ns4:content height="648" medium="image" type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1308927414-1152x648.jpg" width="1152">
<ns4:thumbnail height="500" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1308927414-500x500.jpg" width="500" />
<ns4:credit>Tashi-Delek</ns4:credit></ns4:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Neanderthal wooden tools from Spain found preserved in stone</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/neanderthal-wooden-tools-from-spain-found-preserved-in-stone/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/neanderthal-wooden-tools-from-spain-found-preserved-in-stone/#comments</comments>
                
                <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 22:34:03 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category>Science</category>
		<category>Archeology</category>
		<category>Neanderthals</category>
		<category>woodworking</category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/neanderthal-wooden-tools-from-spain-found-preserved-in-stone/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        Dissolved rock precipitated around the tools, which then decayed.
                    </description>
                                                                <ns2:encoded>
                            &lt;p&gt;The Stone Age owes its name to tools that survived to our time. The material early humans worked with most, though, was probably wood because it is so much easier to process. Unfortunately, we know very little about Paleolithic woodwork because wood rots relatively qu</ns2:encoded>
                                    
                                    <ns3:comments>69</ns3:comments>
                
                
                <ns4:content height="648" medium="image" type="image/png" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/image-1152x648.png" width="1152">
<ns4:thumbnail height="500" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/image-500x500.png" width="500" />
<ns4:credit>Gerard Campeny / IPHES-CERCA</ns4:credit></ns4:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Ukraine’s drones knock out AI data center belonging to "Russia’s Google"</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drones-knock-out-ai-data-center-belonging-to-russias-google/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drones-knock-out-ai-data-center-belonging-to-russias-google/#comments</comments>
                
                <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 22:04:07 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category>AI</category>
		<category>Tech</category>
		<category>ai data centers</category>
		<category>data centers</category>
		<category>drones</category>
		<category>military drones</category>
		<category>russia</category>
		<category>Russia invasion of Ukraine</category>
		<category>Russia war on Ukraine</category>
		<category>Russian invasion of Ukraine</category>
		<category>Russian war on Ukraine</category>
		<category>Ukraine</category>
		<category>Ukraine war</category>
		<category>yandex</category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drones-knock-out-ai-data-center-belonging-to-russias-google/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        One damaged data center has supercomputers used for training Yandex’s AI model.
                    </description>
                                                                <ns2:encoded>
                            &lt;p&gt;Ukrainian drone strikes have knocked out two of five data centers belonging to the Russian tech giant Yandex. The damage has left Yandex, known as “Russia’s Google” because it operates the country’s most popular search engine, struggling to maintain its AI chatbot and</ns2:encoded>
                                    
                                    <ns3:comments>99</ns3:comments>
                
                
                <ns4:content medium="image" type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/yandex-data-center_sasovo-ryazan-russia.jpg">
<ns4:thumbnail height="500" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/yandex-data-center_sasovo-ryazan-russia-500x500.jpg" width="500" />
<ns4:credit>Planet Labs PBC</ns4:credit><ns4:text>A satellite image showing damage to an AI data center owned by the Russian tech giant Yandex in the Sasovo region of Russia.</ns4:text></ns4:content>
            </item>
